WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.667 --> 00:00:03.567
♪♪

00:00:03.567 --> 00:00:05.567
[ Bombastic fanfare plays ]

00:00:05.567 --> 00:00:15.567
♪♪

00:00:15.567 --> 00:00:25.567
♪♪

00:00:25.567 --> 00:00:28.601
♪♪

00:00:28.601 --> 00:00:32.100
[ Laughter ]

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:36.100
♪♪

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:37.901
[ Music stops ]

00:00:37.901 --> 00:00:40.901
[ Laughter ]

00:00:44.534 --> 00:00:47.701
And here I was worried
that was gonna look weird.

00:00:47.701 --> 00:00:50.501
[ Laughter ]

00:00:50.501 --> 00:00:52.100
[ Clears throat ]

00:00:57.801 --> 00:01:01.400
Welcome to the Specjackular,
everybody.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:05.701
A celebration of 50 episodes
of "Corrections."

00:01:07.601 --> 00:01:09.767
[ Laughter ]

00:01:09.767 --> 00:01:11.701
We discussed last week,
we didn't know

00:01:11.701 --> 00:01:14.100
if we were going to go
with Specjackular

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:17.000
or "Jack-tacular" as a name.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.968
We decided
on Specjackular --

00:01:18.968 --> 00:01:24.100
"Jack-tacular," I remembered,
was a title in that metal binder

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:26.200
we had at Maloney Video.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:28.734
That's a callback.
Here's the thing.

00:01:28.734 --> 00:01:32.334
Tonight's gonna be
a lot of callbacks.

00:01:32.334 --> 00:01:33.834
If this is your
first time with us,

00:01:33.834 --> 00:01:37.200
you're gonna want to leave
and catch up.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.400
[ Laughter ]

00:01:42.767 --> 00:01:45.234
I also need to stress, and I
really want to establish this

00:01:45.234 --> 00:01:47.400
right off the top,
the Specjackular --

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:49.601
This is not
an episode of "Corrections."

00:01:49.601 --> 00:01:52.634
This is a celebration
of 50 episodes of "Corrections."

00:01:52.634 --> 00:01:54.834
There's a real
distinction there.

00:01:54.834 --> 00:01:56.834
We're gonna -- Obviously,
we're gonna have corrections,

00:01:56.834 --> 00:01:59.300
but we're going to have
a ton of other stuff

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:02.868
that you're not used to seeing
in an episode of "Corrections,"

00:02:02.868 --> 00:02:07.434
because "Corrections,"
to me, is like...

00:02:07.434 --> 00:02:11.534
If I had to categorize it,
it would be a short-form comedy,

00:02:11.534 --> 00:02:13.167
drama, or variety series.

00:02:13.167 --> 00:02:16.033
[ Laughter ]

00:02:16.033 --> 00:02:19.534
Whereas the Specjackular,

00:02:19.534 --> 00:02:22.334
if it was anything,
would be like, uh...

00:02:22.334 --> 00:02:24.734
variety special, pre-recorded.

00:02:24.734 --> 00:02:32.834
[ Laughter ]

00:02:32.834 --> 00:02:38.234
Um, it is -- you guys,
it is Emmy-voting season,

00:02:38.234 --> 00:02:40.200
so if any of you jackals
are registered

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:42.167
Emmy voters, I want to stress,

00:02:42.167 --> 00:02:46.267
I don't think you guys
should be able to vote on stuff.

00:02:46.267 --> 00:02:47.400
Like, not just Emmys,

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:51.367
like municipal elections --
just in general.

00:02:55.367 --> 00:02:58.834
Hmm.
Oh!

00:02:58.834 --> 00:03:00.234
[ Laughter ]

00:03:00.234 --> 00:03:02.200
What's that?
Oh, you guys, this is exciting.

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:04.200
I feel like we might have a --

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.767
We might have a special message
for the jackals.

00:03:06.767 --> 00:03:08.100
Let's see who it is.

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:10.534
Hello, jackals.
Hey, I'm Jeff Goldblum.

00:03:10.534 --> 00:03:12.267
And I just wanted
to remind you that --

00:03:12.267 --> 00:03:13.701
that I'm up here.

00:03:13.701 --> 00:03:19.167
Yes, yes, yes.
Happy 50th.

00:03:19.167 --> 00:03:22.734
So nice.
Jeff Goldblum.

00:03:22.734 --> 00:03:25.934
I mispronounced
beer koozies last week.

00:03:25.934 --> 00:03:28.934
I thought it was beer "cozies."

00:03:28.934 --> 00:03:32.634
It is a combination
of koozie and cool.

00:03:32.634 --> 00:03:34.801
And I love a good portmanteau.

00:03:34.801 --> 00:03:36.434
You know that about me,
Shoemaker.

00:03:36.434 --> 00:03:39.267
I mean, it's a Specjackular,
after all.

00:03:39.267 --> 00:03:41.968
[ Laughter ]

00:03:41.968 --> 00:03:45.300
We talked about the name
of the vampire Nosferatu.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:46.968
The vampire
was not named Nosferatu.

00:03:46.968 --> 00:03:49.033
He was name Count Orlok.

00:03:49.033 --> 00:03:50.734
And I mentioned that
I should have remembered that

00:03:50.734 --> 00:03:54.534
because of the popular '80s

00:03:54.534 --> 00:03:59.534
General Mills cereal
Counter Chorlokula.

00:03:59.534 --> 00:04:04.100
But then I was told,
"Well, actually, in that,

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:10.033
I think, 1979 version
of "Nosferatu,"

00:04:10.033 --> 00:04:12.000
it was -- he was
actually named Dracula.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:13.501
So in order for
that joke to work,

00:04:13.501 --> 00:04:16.234
I should've gone back
to the 1922

00:04:16.234 --> 00:04:23.033
"Nosferatu" and used
a photo of Max Schreck.

00:04:23.033 --> 00:04:25.300
And so it would've
looked like that.

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:26.968
Max Schreck was a German actor.

00:04:26.968 --> 00:04:29.133
And if you want to see
what he looks like,

00:04:29.133 --> 00:04:33.267
not dressed like Dracula,
here's Max Schreck.

00:04:33.267 --> 00:04:40.534
[ Laughter ]

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:49.767
Ooh. You know what?
I think that's a nice segue

00:04:49.767 --> 00:04:53.367
to our next clip
from a special friend.

00:04:53.367 --> 00:04:57.934
Hello, Chuckles.
My name is Werner Herzog.

00:05:01.834 --> 00:05:03.567
[ Laughter ]

00:05:03.567 --> 00:05:04.601
Okay.
So...

00:05:04.601 --> 00:05:08.467
[ Laughter ]

00:05:08.467 --> 00:05:10.634
Maybe didn't fully understand
the assignment.

00:05:10.634 --> 00:05:14.200
[ Laughter ]

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:16.033
All right.
More than one of you,

00:05:16.033 --> 00:05:18.033
or else I would've let it go...

00:05:18.033 --> 00:05:20.033
I would've let it go.
But more than one of you

00:05:20.033 --> 00:05:27.200
told me "short-lived"
is pronounced short-"lyved."

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:30.534
I mean, if you want to go
through life that way...

00:05:30.534 --> 00:05:31.701
[ Laughter ]

00:05:31.701 --> 00:05:34.033
Lif -- If you want to go
through lif that way?

00:05:34.033 --> 00:05:37.267
[ Laughter ]

00:05:37.267 --> 00:05:38.634
I think we've been really good

00:05:38.634 --> 00:05:40.467
over the course of this,
over 50 episodes,

00:05:40.467 --> 00:05:41.734
if anybody wanted
to say anything,

00:05:41.734 --> 00:05:42.767
it's like, "You know what?"

00:05:42.767 --> 00:05:44.968
They don't just
share the praise.

00:05:44.968 --> 00:05:46.968
When people got things
to say about the show

00:05:46.968 --> 00:05:48.601
that are negative,

00:05:48.601 --> 00:05:50.067
they give them a platform, too.

00:05:50.067 --> 00:05:52.701
Well, here's something we got.

00:05:52.701 --> 00:05:54.434
And again,
I don't enjoy reading them,

00:05:54.434 --> 00:05:57.200
but I think it's important,
if somebody took the time

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:00.367
to criticize our show,
I want to share it with you.

00:06:00.367 --> 00:06:03.634
"Seth, you were doing
something abysmally stupid.

00:06:03.634 --> 00:06:05.200
You were doing a national show,

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:08.701
and you were playing
to the guys on your crew.

00:06:08.701 --> 00:06:10.767
You were making references
to local psychology,

00:06:10.767 --> 00:06:12.901
local sports trends,
local inside jokes.

00:06:12.901 --> 00:06:14.934
You were not doing
all of these things

00:06:14.934 --> 00:06:16.601
in this particular episode,
but it has been a problem

00:06:16.601 --> 00:06:18.200
in the past.
Your quandary is obvious,

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:19.901
and I'm sorry
for this limitation.

00:06:19.901 --> 00:06:23.834
I wish you the best,
no matter what."

00:06:23.834 --> 00:06:25.968
He sounds like a real Paul.

00:06:25.968 --> 00:06:34.501
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:06:38.601 --> 00:06:40.300
We mentioned
it's Emmy-voting season,

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:44.267
and if there's any show --
if there's any show

00:06:44.267 --> 00:06:45.567
we here at "Late Night" feel

00:06:45.567 --> 00:06:48.267
has been overlooked
in multiple Emmy seasons,

00:06:48.267 --> 00:06:51.834
it is
"Tiny Secret Whispers."

00:06:51.834 --> 00:06:53.601
[ Laughter ]

00:06:53.601 --> 00:06:55.901
So you can just imagine
how excited we were

00:06:55.901 --> 00:06:57.334
to receive this video.

00:06:57.334 --> 00:07:01.234
-What's up, all you "TSW" fans.
This is Joel Edgerton.

00:07:01.234 --> 00:07:03.968
[ Laughter ]

00:07:03.968 --> 00:07:07.000
Wishing all you jackals
a happy 50th

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:10.133
and, uh...

00:07:10.133 --> 00:07:13.000
"Tiny Secret Whispers,"
I know you're part of that.

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:14.701
-Oh.

00:07:14.701 --> 00:07:17.100
And you can tell that's
Joel Edgerton

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:19.501
and not the actor he's often
confused for --

00:07:19.501 --> 00:07:21.367
Joel "Edge-erton."

00:07:21.367 --> 00:07:23.767
Because Joel "Edge-erton"
is an accomplished filmmaker

00:07:23.767 --> 00:07:26.200
and director,
and I don't think he ever

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:28.334
would have sent in something
that looked like that.

00:07:28.334 --> 00:07:33.667
[ Laughter ]

00:07:33.667 --> 00:07:38.300
Hey, "Corrections" began...

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:40.534
Not to origin story this
too much.

00:07:40.534 --> 00:07:43.400
But it is the Specjackular.

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:45.701
...because I said Legos
instead of Lego bricks.

00:07:45.701 --> 00:07:49.834
And, you know, I have referenced
this time and time again,

00:07:49.834 --> 00:07:53.100
and some might say
I'm still stuck up on it.

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:55.601
And one of you said
something very sweet.

00:07:55.601 --> 00:07:58.834
This is a jackal's comment.

00:07:58.834 --> 00:08:00.901
You said,
"You've beat yourself up

00:08:00.901 --> 00:08:02.534
over that mistake for too long.

00:08:02.534 --> 00:08:04.968
It's time to lego."

00:08:04.968 --> 00:08:10.734
[ Laughter ]

00:08:10.734 --> 00:08:13.200
We discussed if the Titanic
was a ship or a boat.

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:15.534
One of you pointed out,
"Technically it's a submarine."

00:08:15.534 --> 00:08:16.801
I was told...

00:08:16.801 --> 00:08:22.734
[ Laughter ]

00:08:22.734 --> 00:08:27.167
I pronounced Tintin --
when I tried to pronounce it,

00:08:27.167 --> 00:08:31.033
in French, "Tawn-tawn" --
"Tahn-tahn"?

00:08:31.033 --> 00:08:34.667
Well, I was told "Tawn-tawn"
sounds like the ice --

00:08:34.667 --> 00:08:36.734
you know, the ice planet Hoth
in "Empire Strikes Back,"

00:08:36.734 --> 00:08:38.567
there's a character
called a tauntaun.

00:08:38.567 --> 00:08:41.167
You guys remember
what it looks like.

00:08:41.167 --> 00:08:42.934
Guys remember that?

00:08:42.934 --> 00:08:49.067
[ Laughter ]

00:08:49.067 --> 00:08:50.467
So, I -- you know,

00:08:50.467 --> 00:08:53.467
the last few weeks,
few episodes of "Corrections,"

00:08:53.467 --> 00:08:55.501
we've been hung up on
French words I'm saying wrong

00:08:55.501 --> 00:08:57.667
and the fact
that my mom is a French teacher.

00:08:57.667 --> 00:09:01.300
And I've done this faint...

00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:04.434
"I got a video from my mom,"
and it has not been my mom.

00:09:04.434 --> 00:09:08.234
It has been a clip
of William Shatner

00:09:08.234 --> 00:09:11.133
yelling "Khan" from
the "Star Trek" film

00:09:11.133 --> 00:09:12.734
"The Wrath of Khan."

00:09:12.734 --> 00:09:13.968
And -- But you know what?

00:09:13.968 --> 00:09:16.567
This is not a normal
episodes of "Corrections."

00:09:16.567 --> 00:09:19.200
This is a Specjackular.
I feel like it'd be very special

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:21.567
to have the real
Hillary Meyers here.

00:09:21.567 --> 00:09:24.834
And this all started
because I was -- I did not know

00:09:24.834 --> 00:09:29.701
how to pronounce
the name of the French city...

00:09:29.701 --> 00:09:32.100
"Can," "Con"?
Let's find out. Mom?

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:35.033
-[ William Shatner's voice ]
Khan!

00:09:35.033 --> 00:09:40.501
[ Laughter ]

00:09:40.501 --> 00:09:42.801
You know what's really cool
about it?

00:09:42.801 --> 00:09:45.968
I sent my mom and my dad
instructions for what I wanted,

00:09:45.968 --> 00:09:49.234
and they got it
the first time.

00:09:49.234 --> 00:09:55.634
[ Laughter ]

00:09:55.634 --> 00:09:57.534
No back and forth.
No...

00:09:57.534 --> 00:10:00.667
[ Laughter ]

00:10:00.667 --> 00:10:04.167
No videos with, "This?"
question mark.

00:10:04.167 --> 00:10:06.267
'Cause I'd feel terrible.

00:10:06.267 --> 00:10:07.734
That would rip
that marriage apart

00:10:07.734 --> 00:10:09.968
if they didn't get it
right on the first try.

00:10:13.434 --> 00:10:14.767
We did a segment about bees.

00:10:14.767 --> 00:10:16.434
I received a lot
of bee corrections.

00:10:16.434 --> 00:10:18.400
I attempted to correct
the record on bees.

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:20.367
That started a round
of counter corrections.

00:10:20.367 --> 00:10:22.934
I just want to show you
kind of what I'm up against.

00:10:22.934 --> 00:10:24.701
So, again, I went out of my way

00:10:24.701 --> 00:10:27.701
to get all the bee stuff
right after we had it corrected,

00:10:27.701 --> 00:10:29.968
and -- and someone sent this in.
It says,

00:10:29.968 --> 00:10:31.734
"Bees don't die
when they lose their stingers.

00:10:31.734 --> 00:10:33.968
That's wasps. Whoever posted
that comment, Seth,

00:10:33.968 --> 00:10:36.133
they sit on a throne of lies.
Truly, there's nothing more --"

00:10:36.133 --> 00:10:38.801
And I just want to point
this interesting fact out.

00:10:38.801 --> 00:10:41.434
Guys, I can't.
73 replies.

00:10:41.434 --> 00:10:44.601
I'm not gonna read
all of those bee comments.

00:10:44.601 --> 00:10:46.100
I think we just got
to let it go

00:10:46.100 --> 00:10:49.100
and agree we're just never
gonna understand bees.

00:10:49.100 --> 00:10:50.400
[ Laughter ]

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:52.400
Good news is,
from what I keep reading,

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:53.901
we're not gonna be
around that long.

00:10:53.901 --> 00:10:55.501
[ Laughter ]

00:10:55.501 --> 00:10:58.634
Which I guess is bad news.

00:10:58.634 --> 00:11:00.901
Even the good news
is bad news now.

00:11:00.901 --> 00:11:04.501
It's like, "Hooray! No bees!"
"Actually..."

00:11:04.501 --> 00:11:08.167
[ Laughter ]

00:11:08.167 --> 00:11:10.901
[ Humming ]

00:11:14.367 --> 00:11:16.133
This is --
again, I love when

00:11:16.133 --> 00:11:17.467
the jackals
jackal each other.

00:11:17.467 --> 00:11:20.667
Again, this first comment's
so lovely.

00:11:20.667 --> 00:11:22.868
"These are getting better
every week like fine wine.

00:11:22.868 --> 00:11:25.133
I genuinely laughed out loud
multiple times,

00:11:25.133 --> 00:11:27.133
and I rarely do that
with late-night shows.

00:11:27.133 --> 00:11:29.567
Great job.
These jokes are wonderful."

00:11:29.567 --> 00:11:32.033
Follow up,
from the next jackal --

00:11:32.033 --> 00:11:34.100
"Correction -- most wines
don't actually get better

00:11:34.100 --> 00:11:35.434
with age once bottled.

00:11:35.434 --> 00:11:40.334
At some point, most wines
will deteriorate and go bad."

00:11:40.334 --> 00:11:42.767
So that praise
was short "lyved."

00:11:42.767 --> 00:11:44.601
[ Laughter ]

00:11:44.601 --> 00:11:47.234
Yeah. You know what?
It does sound right.

00:11:47.234 --> 00:11:51.601
[ Laughter ]

00:11:51.601 --> 00:11:53.534
You know, we made more friends
than enemies

00:11:53.534 --> 00:11:54.901
during "Corrections,"
and I think that's

00:11:54.901 --> 00:11:56.367
one of the nicest things
about it.

00:11:56.367 --> 00:11:59.167
But the greatest journey
has been the friends

00:11:59.167 --> 00:12:01.200
we've made of former enemies.

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:03.834
I ran afoul of
the knitting community

00:12:03.834 --> 00:12:07.000
because I failed to distinguish
between knitting and sewing,

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:09.634
and I tried to make amends.

00:12:09.634 --> 00:12:12.200
And I'm very happy to say
the knitting community

00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:13.567
has since embraced me.

00:12:13.567 --> 00:12:15.734
And one knitter in particular,
Patty Lyons,

00:12:15.734 --> 00:12:18.067
has sent some wonderful
sweaters, hats.

00:12:18.067 --> 00:12:22.167
And on top of all that,
she sent us this message.

00:12:22.167 --> 00:12:25.701
-This is Patty Lyons
with a quick reminder for Seth

00:12:25.701 --> 00:12:31.100
that sewing is not knitting.

00:12:31.100 --> 00:12:32.634
And if he makes
that mistake again,

00:12:32.634 --> 00:12:36.000
we'll kill him in his sleep.

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:37.601
Happy 50th!

00:12:39.334 --> 00:12:43.534
[ Laughter ]

00:12:43.534 --> 00:12:46.067
-We talked about Paul Revere,
how it wasn't his horse.

00:12:46.067 --> 00:12:47.767
He borrowed it for the ride
and never returned it.

00:12:47.767 --> 00:12:52.000
And for that,
the wokes are canceling him.

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:53.934
Not allowed to talk
about Paul Revere anymore.

00:12:53.934 --> 00:12:55.200
But here's the reason
you shouldn't talk about

00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:58.300
Paul Revere anymore.
You dig into it, Paul Revere

00:12:58.300 --> 00:13:02.901
did not finish
the ride to Concord. Okay?

00:13:02.901 --> 00:13:09.968
You look it up. It wasn't him.
It was -- It was Prescott.

00:13:09.968 --> 00:13:12.133
Prescott did it, not Revere.

00:13:12.133 --> 00:13:16.734
But then old,
old Henry Wadsworth Longfellow

00:13:16.734 --> 00:13:20.367
writes a poem called
"Paul Revere's Ride,"

00:13:20.367 --> 00:13:21.701
and Paul Revere
just historically

00:13:21.701 --> 00:13:25.534
then gets all the credit.

00:13:25.534 --> 00:13:27.767
'Cause here's the beginning
of the poem.

00:13:27.767 --> 00:13:29.701
"Listen, my children,
and you shall hear

00:13:29.701 --> 00:13:31.033
of the midnight
ride of Paul Revere."

00:13:31.033 --> 00:13:33.334
So, basically, Paul Revere
gets all the credit

00:13:33.334 --> 00:13:36.234
because his name rhymes
with "hear."

00:13:36.234 --> 00:13:37.334
[ Laughter ]

00:13:37.334 --> 00:13:41.033
And Longfellow -- I'll say it --

00:13:41.033 --> 00:13:44.200
too [bleep] lazy to come up
with a rhyme for Prescott.

00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:47.868
[ Laughter ]

00:13:47.868 --> 00:13:52.801
And just, like,
know that about poets --

00:13:52.801 --> 00:13:57.367
they will rewrite history
for the rhyme.

00:14:00.534 --> 00:14:02.067
"Listen up, children,
here's what I got."

00:14:02.067 --> 00:14:03.934
Ugh.

00:14:03.934 --> 00:14:06.400
[ Laughter ]

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:08.167
"Tell you the story,
I'll give it a shot."

00:14:08.167 --> 00:14:12.367
"You know what? I might
just stick with Paul Revere."

00:14:12.367 --> 00:14:16.200
Also, they had to be subtle.

00:14:16.200 --> 00:14:17.434
You know, I think there's
a lot of like,

00:14:17.434 --> 00:14:19.133
"The British are coming,
the British are coming!"

00:14:19.133 --> 00:14:20.901
I think that's
how we remember it,

00:14:20.901 --> 00:14:22.834
or we've been told
to remember it.

00:14:22.834 --> 00:14:24.667
But, you know,
British were everywhere.

00:14:24.667 --> 00:14:26.334
So I was told
that that is incorrect.

00:14:26.334 --> 00:14:28.067
They had to be
really subtle about it.

00:14:28.067 --> 00:14:29.601
So it was a lot of like,

00:14:29.601 --> 00:14:32.267
"Oh, Paul, what are you doing
on a midnight ride?"

00:14:32.267 --> 00:14:34.133
It's like, "Oh, I thought
I'd take the horse out

00:14:34.133 --> 00:14:36.000
under the moonlight.
The British are coming."

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:37.434
There was a lot of that.

00:14:37.434 --> 00:14:38.467
[ Laughter ]

00:14:38.467 --> 00:14:39.601
"What was that?"

00:14:39.601 --> 00:14:41.834
"Oh, just borrowed
the Deacon's horse...

00:14:41.834 --> 00:14:45.467
The British coming.
...and might not return it.

00:14:45.467 --> 00:14:46.667
British are coming."

00:14:46.667 --> 00:14:48.400
[ Laughter ]

00:14:48.400 --> 00:14:49.901
Hey, do you guys like Broadway?

00:14:49.901 --> 00:14:51.934
If so, I think
you'll enjoy this message

00:14:51.934 --> 00:14:53.901
from a cast of legends.

00:14:53.901 --> 00:14:55.701
-Hello, jackals.

00:14:55.701 --> 00:14:59.400
This is Nathan Lane coming
to you live from rehearsals

00:14:59.400 --> 00:15:02.634
for the soon to be hit
Broadway musical

00:15:02.634 --> 00:15:04.367
"Cicada, Cicada."

00:15:04.367 --> 00:15:08.234
I just want to wish you
all a happy 50th episode.

00:15:08.234 --> 00:15:12.200
And I also want to fire my agent
for convincing me to do this.

00:15:12.200 --> 00:15:15.133
-Hello, jackals.
This is Christine Baranski,

00:15:15.133 --> 00:15:19.834
coming to you during rehearsals
of "Cicada, Cicada."

00:15:19.834 --> 00:15:23.033
It's not going well.
Um, there's no second act.

00:15:23.033 --> 00:15:27.100
And the songs are catchy
but in a bad way,

00:15:27.100 --> 00:15:32.634
like chlamydia.
Anyway...happy 50th.

00:15:32.634 --> 00:15:34.467
-Hi, jackals.

00:15:34.467 --> 00:15:38.100
It's Harvey Fierstein
from "Cicada, Cicada."

00:15:38.100 --> 00:15:43.133
Rehearsals are a nightmare.
Nathan and Christine are a lot,

00:15:43.133 --> 00:15:46.467
and being "a lot"
is my thing.

00:15:46.467 --> 00:15:50.067
Anyway, happy 50th.

00:15:50.067 --> 00:15:52.033
[ Laughter ]

00:15:52.033 --> 00:15:54.334
Very passive-aggressive
of Ms. Baranski

00:15:54.334 --> 00:15:56.667
to pronounce the title wrong.

00:15:56.667 --> 00:15:59.968
[ Laughter ]

00:15:59.968 --> 00:16:03.434
When I got into late night,

00:16:03.434 --> 00:16:08.334
it was not for the fame
or the money

00:16:08.334 --> 00:16:12.067
or because I like
telling jokes or comedy at all,

00:16:12.067 --> 00:16:13.868
or that I liked coming
to this building,

00:16:13.868 --> 00:16:16.033
or I like the people
I worked with,

00:16:16.033 --> 00:16:19.701
or that I liked having something
to do in the afternoon,

00:16:19.701 --> 00:16:21.801
or that I liked people
recognizing me on the street.

00:16:21.801 --> 00:16:26.367
It wasn't for any of that.
I got into late night

00:16:26.367 --> 00:16:30.067
because I wanted my own
Ben &amp; Jerry's flavor.

00:16:30.067 --> 00:16:32.701
That was it.
[ Laughter ]

00:16:32.701 --> 00:16:37.067
So you can only imagine...

00:16:37.067 --> 00:16:42.167
how I felt...
when I saw this.

00:16:42.167 --> 00:16:46.167
-Hey, Seth, it's Ben.
-And Jerry, congratulating you

00:16:46.167 --> 00:16:49.934
on your 50th episode
of "Corrections."

00:16:49.934 --> 00:16:51.968
-And to honor, your achievement,

00:16:51.968 --> 00:16:55.434
we're happy to announce
our newest flavor.

00:16:55.434 --> 00:16:59.133
-James Corden's
Carpool Choclaoke.

00:16:59.133 --> 00:17:00.868
-Whoo!

00:17:00.868 --> 00:17:06.767
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:17:11.734 --> 00:17:13.767
The bummer is, they sent it,
and it's really good.

00:17:13.767 --> 00:17:17.868
[ Laughter ]

00:17:21.934 --> 00:17:24.167
We had a dream list

00:17:24.167 --> 00:17:28.567
of guest stars
for the Specjackular.

00:17:28.567 --> 00:17:32.167
And when this person said yes...

00:17:32.167 --> 00:17:35.267
Mm.

00:17:35.267 --> 00:17:39.968
To get an Oscar winner,
to get a legend...

00:17:39.968 --> 00:17:41.767
Ladies and gentlemen...

00:17:41.767 --> 00:17:43.067
Al Pacino.

00:17:43.067 --> 00:17:44.968
-Hey, jackals,
this is Al Pacino,

00:17:44.968 --> 00:17:47.167
coming to you live
from the Zoom Room.

00:17:47.167 --> 00:17:48.567
I want to wish you a happy 50th

00:17:48.567 --> 00:17:53.067
and to remember
to never stop jackalin'.

00:17:53.067 --> 00:17:55.367
How was that?

00:17:55.367 --> 00:17:57.901
What do you mean
the camera was off?

00:17:57.901 --> 00:18:00.334
Oh, man.
Go --

00:18:00.334 --> 00:18:02.434
Well, they'll know it's me.

00:18:02.434 --> 00:18:05.501
Who else they gonna think it is?
The guy from "Barry"?

00:18:05.501 --> 00:18:07.667
Come on, give me a break.

00:18:07.667 --> 00:18:12.167
[ Laughter ]

00:18:12.167 --> 00:18:15.968
Goose bumps.

00:18:15.968 --> 00:18:17.200
Um...

00:18:17.200 --> 00:18:20.434
I want to make some thank-yous
real quick

00:18:20.434 --> 00:18:23.133
as we wrap up
the "Jack-tacular" here.

00:18:23.133 --> 00:18:25.701
I want to thank director Alex
and everyone in the booth

00:18:25.701 --> 00:18:27.100
who sticks around
to do this every week.

00:18:27.100 --> 00:18:28.734
Thank you guys so much.
I want to thank

00:18:28.734 --> 00:18:33.033
Anthony and Devon for pulling
and editing the clips together.

00:18:33.033 --> 00:18:36.601
I want to thank "Tar-a" for her
amazing work with the graphics.

00:18:36.601 --> 00:18:39.033
I also want to thank "Teh-ra"
because she won't tell me

00:18:39.033 --> 00:18:40.300
which of those she prefers.

00:18:40.300 --> 00:18:43.067
[ Laughter ]

00:18:43.067 --> 00:18:45.534
20 years in, and it's like,
"Either one's fine."

00:18:45.534 --> 00:18:47.534
I don't believe you,
"Tar-a" or "Teh-ra"...

00:18:47.534 --> 00:18:50.434
[ Laughter ]
...but thank you.

00:18:50.434 --> 00:18:52.601
I want to thank Ellen
and her team

00:18:52.601 --> 00:18:54.767
for pulling props together
for us every week.

00:18:54.767 --> 00:18:56.934
I want to thank Jimmy
for getting everything

00:18:56.934 --> 00:18:58.234
ready backstage.

00:18:58.234 --> 00:19:01.801
I want to thank our
cameraman Buck for his laughs.

00:19:01.801 --> 00:19:05.200
[ Laughter ]
You know.

00:19:05.200 --> 00:19:07.601
Not all the jokes
and corrections are great,

00:19:07.601 --> 00:19:08.834
but Buck can't tell.

00:19:08.834 --> 00:19:11.834
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:19:11.834 --> 00:19:13.367
You should all --

00:19:13.367 --> 00:19:15.701
You should all be so lucky
to have a Buck in your life.

00:19:15.701 --> 00:19:17.534
[ Laughter ]

00:19:17.534 --> 00:19:19.400
Want to thank Eric and Donna
for helping me with

00:19:19.400 --> 00:19:22.467
all my wardrobe needs.
I'd like to thank Pizza Hut.

00:19:22.467 --> 00:19:25.167
I'd like to,
but since we reached out to see

00:19:25.167 --> 00:19:27.367
if they actually wanted to pay
some money for a sponsorship,

00:19:27.367 --> 00:19:28.834
crickets.

00:19:28.834 --> 00:19:33.667
[ Laughter ]

00:19:33.667 --> 00:19:35.667
I don't need to thank Wally.

00:19:35.667 --> 00:19:38.934
No cue cards for "Corrections,"
and we do just fine.

00:19:38.934 --> 00:19:42.501
[ Laughter ]

00:19:42.501 --> 00:19:44.100
I want to thank Lederman
for helping get this up

00:19:44.100 --> 00:19:45.133
and online every week.

00:19:45.133 --> 00:19:46.300
I want to thank
all of our friends

00:19:46.300 --> 00:19:50.434
who sent in videos
for this Specjackular.

00:19:50.434 --> 00:19:52.167
What a --
What a dear group of people.

00:19:52.167 --> 00:19:57.133
And, of course,
I want to thank...

00:19:57.133 --> 00:20:01.601
I want to thank the jackals,
because without you...

00:20:01.601 --> 00:20:03.234
this would -- and I know
it's hard to believe --

00:20:03.234 --> 00:20:05.467
be even more meaningless.

00:20:05.467 --> 00:20:07.868
[ Laughter ]

00:20:07.868 --> 00:20:11.200
And...I think that's --

00:20:11.200 --> 00:20:13.100
I think that's about everyone.

00:20:13.100 --> 00:20:14.534
[ Static ] --
What? What's hap--

00:20:14.534 --> 00:20:16.567
What's happening?
What is this?

00:20:16.567 --> 00:20:24.334
[ All cheering ]

00:20:28.467 --> 00:20:32.000
Hey, jackals,
I'm on the Amystika stage,

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:35.634
and I got one question
for all of you.

00:20:35.634 --> 00:20:37.501
Is this your card?

00:20:37.501 --> 00:20:40.801
-9 of diamonds!

00:20:40.801 --> 00:20:44.601
[ All cheering ]

00:20:46.033 --> 00:20:50.601
[ Laughter ]

00:20:53.501 --> 00:20:55.200
I mean...

00:20:55.200 --> 00:20:59.834
[ Laughter ]

00:20:59.834 --> 00:21:02.167
Mindfreak.

00:21:02.167 --> 00:21:03.734
[ Scoffs ]

00:21:05.701 --> 00:21:08.267
[ Laughter ]

00:21:08.267 --> 00:21:10.000
I can't believe it.

00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:15.067
[ Laughter ]

00:21:15.067 --> 00:21:16.667
I got mindfreaked.

00:21:16.667 --> 00:21:19.801
[ Laughter ]

00:21:22.934 --> 00:21:28.601
[ Laughter ]

00:21:28.601 --> 00:21:30.100
See me next week.

00:21:30.100 --> 00:21:31.300
[ Laughter ]

